    "evidence_text": "BACKGROUND Statins are commonly used against arteriosclerotic disease, but recent retrospective analyses have suggested that statins also prevent cancer.\n\nOnly in vitro studies that discuss the effect of statins on head and neck carcinoma were selected.   \n\n\nRESULTS Of 153 identified papers, 14 studies met the inclusion criteria.\n\nIt provides highlights about the biological mechanisms of statins used alone or associated with traditional therapy for cancer.   \n\n\nStatin prophylaxis and inflammatory mediators following cardiopulmonary bypass: a systematic review\n\nDescriptive analysis was used when data could not be pooled.   \n\n\nSeveral of the trials were not methodologically rigorous and statin intervention was highly variable in this small number of studies.\n\nHMG-CoA reductase inhibitors and the risk of hip fractures in elderly patients.\n\nOBJECTIVE To determine whether the use of statins is associated with reduced hip fracture risk.   \n\n\nDESIGN Case-control study.   \n\n\nCase patients (n=1222) underwent surgical repair of a hip fracture in 1994.\n\nNo significant relationship was observed between use of nonstatin lipid-lowering agents and hip fracture risk.\n\nClear relationships were observed between the degree of reduction in hip fracture risk and the extent of statin use; there was no evidence of such relationships with nonstatin lipid-lowering agents.\n\nUse of nonstatin lipid-lowering agents was not observed to be associated with reduction in hip fracture risk in any of these alternative models or analyses.   \n\n\nCONCLUSIONS These findings support an association between statin use by elderly patients and reduction in the risk of hip fracture.\n\nJAMA.\n\nDESIGN Meta-analysis of randomised trials.   \n\n\nHeterogeneity was assessed using the Q and I(2) statistics.\n\nPublication bias was assessed by visual examination of funnel plots and the Egger regression test.   \n\n\nMean follow-up was 4.1 years.\n\nNo evidence of an increased risk of cancer was observed.\n\nThere was no significant heterogeneity of the treatment effect in clinical subgroups.   \n\n\nStatins in the primary prevention of cardiovascular disease\n\nStatins are widely used in the evidence-based lowering of cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk.\n\nSecondly, long-term statin use might cause adverse effects, such as incident diabetes mellitus.\n\nWhether primary prevention is beneficial in individuals at low or moderate risk is not certain.\n\nTherefore, the prescription of statins for primary prevention should be individualized on the basis of clinical judgment, particularly for low-risk individuals.\n\nPreoperative statin treatment reduces systemic inflammatory response and myocardial damage in cardiac surgery.\n\nPleiotropic effects of statins.\n\nStatins are potent inhibitors of cholesterol biosynthesis.\n\nIn clinical trials, statins are beneficial in the primary and secondary prevention of coronary heart disease.\n\nFurthermore, statins have beneficial extrahepatic effects on the immune system, CNS, and bone.\n\nStatin use and risk of gallstone disease followed by cholecystectomy.\n\nData on this association in humans are scarce.   \n\n\nCONCLUSION Long-term use of statins was associated with a decreased risk of gallstones followed by cholecystectomy.\n\nSimvastatin reduces platelet-endocardium adhesion in atrial fibrillation.\n\nMETHODS In vitro study using human tissue, University Hospital (tertiary referral center).\n\nDose-dependent effect of statins on the incidence of acute kidney injury after cardiac surgery.\n\nPropensity scores were utilized to adjust for the differences between the statin and the no-statin treatment groups.\n\nCONCLUSIONS Statin treatment is not associated with a lower incidence of AKI after cardiac surgery.",
